markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Prompting Basics: Build a Better Prompt

Strong prompts do not usually happen all at once. They get stronger when students slow down, add missing details, and think carefully about what they want the AI to do.

---

## Part 1: Build a Prompt

Start with a vague prompt. Click each section to see how the prompt improves.

<div style="border:3px solid #ccc;border-radius:16px;padding:18px;margin:24px 0;background:#fff;">

  <p style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Starting Prompt:</strong></p>

  <div style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:18px;margin-bottom:18px;">
    Write a filter function.
  </div>

  <details>
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
      Add Context
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;">
      I am building a JavaScript app that displays social media posts stored in an array of objects.
    </div>
  </details>

  <br>

  <details>
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
      Add Goal
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;">
      I want to create a filter function that only shows posts with more than 100 likes.
    </div>
  </details>

  <br>

  <details>
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
      Add Constraints
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;">
      Use JavaScript and the <code>.filter()</code> method. Keep the code beginner-friendly.
    </div>
  </details>

  <br>

  <details>
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
      Add Output Expectations
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;">
      Provide code I can add to my TODO section and include a short explanation of how it works.
    </div>
  </details>

  <br>

  <details>
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#008C95;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
      Reveal the Full Prompt
    </summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;background:#EAF7F8;border-left:6px solid #008C95;border-radius:12px;padding:18px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      I am building a JavaScript app that displays social media posts stored in an array of objects. I want to create a filter function that only shows posts with more than 100 likes. Use JavaScript and the <code>.filter()</code> method. Keep the code beginner-friendly. Provide code I can add to my TODO section and include a short explanation of how it works.
    </div>
  </details>

</div>

---

## Part 2: Match the Prompt Pieces

Each card below is one part of a strong prompt. Click to reveal which category it belongs to.

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2, 1fr);gap:16px;margin:24px 0;">

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:16px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      “I’m building a JavaScript app that tracks books in an array of objects.”
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:15px;">
      🧠 <strong>Context</strong><br>
      This tells the AI what situation or code the student is working with.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:16px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      “Create a function that adds a new book to the array.”
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:15px;">
      🎯 <strong>Goal</strong><br>
      This tells the AI what the student wants help doing.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:16px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      “Use <code>.push()</code> and keep the code beginner-friendly.”
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:15px;">
      ⚙️ <strong>Constraints</strong><br>
      This gives the AI rules or limits to follow.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:16px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      “Include an example of calling the function and explain it with short comments.”
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:15px;">
      📤 <strong>Output Expectations</strong><br>
      This tells the AI what a useful response should include.
    </div>
  </details>

</div>
---

## Teacher Reflection

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5;border-radius:30px;color:#000000;width:95%;padding:18px 24px;margin:24px 0;">

  <p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
    <strong>💡 Reflection:</strong> Which part of the prompting process do you think students are most likely to skip: context, goal, constraints, or output expectations? Why?
  </p>

</div>

<div style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:16px;padding:18px;margin-top:18px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
  <strong>Facilitator Note:</strong> Students may naturally jump to the goal, such as “write a filter function,” but often leave out the context, constraints, or output expectations that help the AI generate a useful response. This is where teachers can slow students down and help them make their thinking more visible.
</div>

---

As you work through this module, pay attention to your prompting process and consider where students may struggle and how you can support them through it.


MARKDOWN
